  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nIdentification of a mid-level vort max moving over the region\nnow plus a second one trailing in northern IL has resulted in\nfar greater cloud coverage and light rain showers this evening.\nWhile the focus of both remains along the I-80 corridor, light\nrain/sprinkles could reach as far south as the I-68 corridor\nthrough Monday. This newer ideal is bolstered by the 00z \nsounding showing a nearly saturated 750mb-300mb layer over a \nmarginally dry sub-750mb layer, meaning rain with shortwave \npassages (but accumulations are generally less than 0.10\").\nPrevious forecasts struggled capturing this in PoP due to \ntiming/spatial coverage/amounts that, by not overlapping within \na 1 hour timeframe, suppressed probabilities. Examination of 6 \nhr probabilities of greater than 0.01\" (measurable rain) better \nshows the rain potential as percentages range from around 20% to\nupwards of 80%. \n\nThe side effect of these clouds/showers is the potential\nsuppression of heating for Monday.  18z hi- res model data \nhighlights the lack of strong 700mb dry advection (associated \nwith the ridge axis) until near sunset Monday that would be\nneeded to create clearer/longer cloud-free windows. This means \nthe previously discussed heat risk starting Monday is trending \nless likely and is fully contingent on prolonged breaks within \nthe mid to high level clouds to manifest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nWhile confidence has dropped with respect to heat for Monday, it\nremains high for those hot and humid conditions to return \nby Tuesday and persist through mid- week as a ridge axis builds\nacross the Great Lakes over the next 24 hours. As a result, \ntemperatures will climb into the upper-80s and low-90s across \nthe region, with at least three consecutive days of hot and \nhumid weather expected through Wednesday. The probability of \ntemperatures reaching the low-90s ranges from 40-90% during this\ntime period, with probabilities trending higher especially on \nWednesday. \n\nOvernight temperatures will provide little relief, with lows\ngenerally remaining in the upper-60s and low-70s. This\ncombination of warm daytime temperatures and limited overnight\ncooling will contribute to increasing heat stress. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nSubtle variations in shortwave positioning causing alterations\nin expected conditions tonight into Monday will continue to \nleave the mid to late week period in flux in relation to \ntiming/coverage/intensity of potential showers and \nthunderstorms. Broadly, these waves will slowly shift the upper \nridge southward while traversing an increasingly warm and humid \nenvironment. Latest NBM mean SBCAPE values during peak afternoon\nheating hovers anywhere from 1000-2500 J/kg starting Tuesday \nthrough Friday. This basic measure of storm-available energy \nsuggests that a multi-day severe hazard risk can't be ruled out,\nwhich SPC has begun noting with a Marginal Risk highlight for \nTuesday, 15% prob severe outlook for Wednesday/Thursday, plus \nadditional discussion points into Friday. \n\nTo restate the top point, while severe hazard risks are possible\nthrough much of the upcoming week, key details about\ncoverage/timing as well as influences from prior convective\nevolution may be difficult to discern until the morning of (or\npotentially even 6-12 hours before). The best advice is to be\naware of the potential and follow forecast discussions in the\ncoming days to get a sense for where trends raise or lower the\nsevere risk for a given day.",
